we'll begin with this leaders from  around the world of starting two days of  observances to remember the 75th  anniversary of d-day president Trump and  Queen Elizabeth are part of this morning  ceremony in Portsmouth England one of  the main departure points for the world  war two invasion of german-occupied  France German Chancellor Angela Merkel  is also there along with representatives  from all 14 Allied nations that took  part in d-day president Trump read a  prayer that Franklin Franklin Roosevelt  delivered to the nation over the radio  on d-day Anthony Mason is in Normandy  France at a cemetery where thousands of  American troops are buried Anthony good  morning good morning this is in fact my  third time visiting the American  Cemetery here in Normandy which is just  the Omaha Beach is just beyond those  headstones and and honestly I'm always  struck by the gravity and enormity of  what happened here it's almost  incomprehensible behind me you can see  some of the perfectly aligned American  graves there are 9380 headstones here  many of the servicemen buried in this  cemetery died during the d-day invasion  in fact that number 9380 is only a third  of the americans lost in the Battle of  Normandy their headstones are all facing  home toward the US on our way in this  morning we got a sense of some of the  enthusiasm here in Normandy we drove  past military vehicles many of them  flying American and French flags this is  a community that has always embraced and  honored those who sacrificed for their  freedom d-day was the largest military  operation ever attempted more than a  hundred and fifty thousand Allied troops  landed on the beaches of Normandy on  June 6 1944 that marked the start of the  campaign to liberate Nazi occupied  Europe the invasion was initially  planned for 75 years ago today but was  delayed 24 hours because of bad weather  fittingly scattered showers are expected  today as well the events of today and  tomorrow will be one of the last  opportunities to honor those who fought  in World War two with veterans still  alive  of the more than 16 million Americans  who served fewer than half a million are  still with us most well into their 90's  now as many as 100 vets are expected to  be here for tomorrow morning ceremony  which is expected to last just over an  hour the President and First Lady will  be greeted by Frances president and  Manuel Mackrell and his wife Brigitte  both leaders will deliver remarks that  will be followed by a moment of silence  and a 21-gun salute  they will then all walk through this  cemetery and participate in a  wreath-laying ceremony
 

